A||||9||LAMBO||oil pressure question||||1||
Z||000000||LAMBO||10-17-2000||11:15 AM||||Ever since the engine swap in my 86 gt, I've noticed that the oil pressure drops to nothing and the warning light comes on whenever I'm hard cornering. As soon as I'm straightened out, it goes right back up to its normal 65 lbs. At idle it runs about 35 lbs. Oil level reads normal on dipstick. Whats up with this. Anyone else experience the same thing. I thought I remembered reading something about this in an earlier post, but I couldn't find it. <BR>Lambo||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||10-17-2000||11:23 AM||||What type of oil are you using? I only use 10W-40. 5W-30 is too light of an oil. Also you say you did an engine swap. What work was done on the new engine? If the oil pick-up in the pan is too high, (or bent up) you will oil starve the engine in hard cornering too. The only other thing I could suggest if you do alot of hard cornering is to baffle the oil pan. this would keep the oil from sloshing away from the pick-up tube. Many race cars use the baffled oil pans for road course racing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LAMBO||10-17-2000||11:34 AM||||Oreif, I didn't have the pan off of the motor. It was a straight swap, no tear down. When I say hard cornering, it really doesn't take much of a corner to throw that needle down to zero and make the light come on. Thats why it seems odd to me. Otherwise, oil pressure is very normal, even at higher temps. I have 5w-30 in it now for winter driving, maybe I should go back to my summer 10w-40 oil. It still shouldn't be doing this, though. Maybe I shouldn't worry about it since it only does this for a few seconds at a time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-17-2000||12:38 PM||||Sounds like an oil pickup problem from the oil pump.  I have heard of this problem and thats why some internal baffles were installed in the oil pan.  I'm unsure of the pickup design.. some sort of tube that has come loose.  Some one in the past switched an oil pan with no baffle?||||reg||3
Z||000004||LAMBO||10-17-2000||12:52 PM||||I don't know any history on this motor other than 103K miles, runs strong and doesn't smoke. I'm leaning towards something wrong on the oil pick up too. Damn, i'm not pulling this motor out again. Going to have to baby it a little until I get the original motor rebuilt this winter. Thanks for the help guys. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||10-17-2000||01:24 PM||||"Sounds like an oil pickup problem from the oil pump." <P>wasn't there just a post about this, oil presure being fine until taking a corner... something about the oil pick up falling off, but the tube is just low enough to keep oil flowing while going straight, but as soon as you turn it uncovers the pipe and sucks air. Anyways, it'd rather pull a pan then go engine shoping.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-18-2000||12:53 AM||||No need to pull the engine for this!<BR>You may need to raise the front of the engine to clear the way for pan removal (and remove the engine hard mount in front).||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierodave||10-18-2000||02:31 AM||||check the grounds from the engine to the frame!!!!!!!  and before you pull anything out of the car  hook up a mechanical guage and check it aginst the highly accurate fiero oil  gauges  lol   had the same problem and it was the ground strap on the engine that had no nut on it and in a corner or on a bump it lost ground<BR>I would check it with a known working  oil pressure gauge first!<BR>just pop out the sender by the battery and hook a gauge to it there!  then you know for sure  have some one sit in the trunk and do your little manuvers to duplicate what happens  lol to read the gauge for you  or tape it up aginst the back glass faceing forward and watch it in your mirror!!!   he he he he <BR>FD||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-18-2000||09:01 PM||||What FD said. the test with the mech gage could save you hours of work for nothing. Always do that first to make sure pressure really drops.<P>don't forget to check the big connector by the battery. that can get tugged durring engine swapping. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||LAMBO||10-18-2000||09:17 PM||||Thanks guys. It may very well be a grounding problem. I've also had an occasional jumping gas gauge, but that seems to have settled down now. Car hadn't been on the road for two years, so I kinda expected some of this stuff to happen. I'll go check grounds.||||reg||1
